\title{Counting lunches, again}
\date{2026-04-29T11:10:22Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\taxon{Example}
\p{You again want to order a single item for lunch; restaurant A has #{13} things on its menu, and Restaurant B has #{24} on its menu. However, there are #{9} items which the two restaurants have in common. How many items can you order for your lunch?}
\p{Adding #{13 + 24} gives the total number of items on both menus, but it has counted the shared items twice. Subtracting the number of shared items gives the correct total: #{13 + 24 - 9 = 28}.}
